# R47 Track B — NEGATIVE CONTROL for the indirect-dispatch call-result class.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE EXISTS TO PROVE THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it is the reason the
# class is not "trait dispatch leaks". Three shapes that DO resolve their
# callee statically, in one program:
#
#   1. a plain free-function call;
#   2. an inherent method on a user struct;
#   3. THE SAME trait method as
#      `indirect_boxtrait_vtable_call_result_leak.gg`, but on a CONCRETE
#      receiver — static dispatch, no vtable.
#
# All three are clean at pristine `f3feea79` and stay clean after the fix.
# Shape 3 is the load-bearing one: it isolates INDIRECTION as the
# discriminator. `t0725` filed this class three times as "unnamed temp" and
# once as "trait dispatch"; both readings are refuted by this file.
#
# ⚠ GREEN-ON-ARRIVAL, DELIBERATELY, AND NO RED WAS AVAILABLE. Being green
# before AND after is precisely what this fixture asserts, so it cannot be
# RED-verified against the pre-fix compiler. Two deliberate breaks were tried
# anyway — registering every chokepoint result twice, and disabling the
# enum/struct ownership transfer — and both left it CLEAN 3/3, for the same
# structural reason the sibling transfer controls record: at these positions a
# second release goes through a slot the runtime has already self-nulled.
# What it pins is the fix's BLAST RADIUS — that the conversion did not sweep
# in statically-dispatched producers and change their value behaviour — and it
# is the fixture that makes the class's discriminator (indirection, not
# traits) checkable rather than asserted.
#
# INSTRUMENT: `security_safe_no_leak`, C lane only.
trait Speaker:
    String speak(self)

struct Robot:
    String id

equip Robot with Speaker:
    String speak(self):
        return self.id + "!"

struct Plain:
    String tag

equip Plain:
    String shout(self):
        return self.tag + "?"

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    print(mk("hel", "lo"))
    Plain p = Plain("t")
    print(p.shout())
    Robot r = Robot("R2")
    print(r.speak())
